## Here's my go at solving the room 'Snapped Phish-ing Line'



## Room Description:
"As a member of the IT department at SwiftSpend Financial, you are responsible for assisting employees with technical concerns. What initially appeared to be a routine day quickly escalated when multiple employees across different departments reported receiving a suspicious email. Several users noted unusual characteristics in the message, and unfortunately, some had already submitted their credentials and were no longer able to access their accounts. With the potential for a wider compromise, the incident has been escalated for investigation. Your task is to analyze the available evidence, determine the scope of the attack, and uncover how the adversary operated."



Objectives:
- Analyze the provided email samples to identify key artifacts
- Investigate phishing URLs to understand redirection
- Retrieve and examine the phishing kit used in the attack
- Use CTI tools to gather intelligence on the adversary
- Analyze the phishing kit to uncover additional indicators

**My Writeup**

So right off the bat, we're given a set of emails to look at. 
![image.png](https://share.jotbird.com/images/b8934671-0599-4719-88f2-50ab7f7f39df.png)

**Q1 - Begin reviewing the emails in the phish-emails folder on your desktop. Which individual recieved the email regarding a **Quote for Services Rendered**?**

Since the question is asking about Services Rendered, we'll start with that file. I opened it both as an .eml file and with Vim to look at the details and see if there was anything of note hiding in it.
![image.png](https://share.jotbird.com/images/f365d5f7-0345-4f5d-8e80-c18de5c02edf.png)
![image.png](https://share.jotbird.com/images/f58dcec2-acda-46be-9f87-3fcf1cdb6f6f.png)
Looks like Mr. McClean was the one to receive it. However a big red flag is how many email servers this passed through. It went through 10 different mail servers and multiple countries traveling from the original sender of mailsvri-04.ssf.thm to our recipient and none of them seem associated with the "sender" or their company.

Ans: William McClean

**Q2 - What email address was used by the adversary to send the phishing emails?**

Ans: Accounts.Payable@groupmarketingonline.icu

**Q3 - Investigate the attachment in the email addressed to Zoe Duncan.
What is the root domain of the redirection URL found within the file?**

So Zoe's email has an attachment in the form of a .html file. When we separate and inspect it, we find the redirect.
![image.png](https://share.jotbird.com/images/f370ff39-f173-4ff3-989b-3140b3f24c90.png)

Ans: kennaroads.buzz

**Q4 - Investigate the attachment in the email addressed to Zoe Duncan.
What is the root domain of the redirection URL found within the file?**

![image.png](https://share.jotbird.com/images/1b17ce16-1a13-4ca9-b0a3-df620f4feca3.png)


And looks just like a Microsoft or Outlook login. Big tells being the website address being kennaroads.buzz, it not being a secured site, and if you do try and interact with the 'Forgot my password' button, nothing happens. Shouldn't be interacting with anything on an obviously fake site, but it's a VM and we can play around.

