**Q9 - Let’s see if the attacker has exposed any captured credentials.
Navigate to the /data/Update365/ directory and investigate the log file.
What is the email address of the user who submitted their credentials more than once?**

Using the same log file posted under Question 5, we can find that Michael Ascot submitted his credentials twice, once at 10:00 AM and 10:01 AM.

Ans: michael.ascot@swiftspend.finance

**Q10 - Extract the phishing kit archive and locate the submit.php file.
What email address is used by the adversary to collect compromised credentials?**

![image.png](https://share.jotbird.com/images/4cae2c30-ce86-4f5f-bbc3-059c74ddff23.png)

Here's why we saw so many random countries popping up for the email servers it was filtered through. They're using a javascript to route though fake emails and countries picked at arandom by geoplugin.net. But the email it's being sent to is found on line 49 - m3npat@yandex.com

Ans: m3npat@yandex.com

**Q11 - Return to the phishing URL and locate the flag.txt file.
Using CyberChef to decode the flag, what is the secret value?**

Going back to the phishing site, we navigate back to the directory. In the ZIP folder and in the directories that are showing, there doesn't seem to be a flag.txt file anywhere - which means it's likely hidden. After poking around a bit with various URLs, we finally find one hidden at office365/flag.txt 
![image.png](https://share.jotbird.com/images/5cc15b68-d954-435d-9cfb-1bab1d827f2b.png)

Now to Cyberchef to decrypt the hidden flag. Using the magic recipe to tell us what type of encryption it likely is, we find that it's probably Base85

![image.png](https://share.jotbird.com/images/73b9adc8-74f9-4b6b-841d-a7aaf2d17395.png)
![image.png](https://share.jotbird.com/images/5bb5297d-025e-4e69-b84b-5201ac39e01c.png)

Hmm, looks like Base85 isn't working. Let's try Base64

![image.png](https://share.jotbird.com/images/5046e022-aac8-4f27-80b6-e9586190dc3f.png)

That looks like our flag, just reversed. If we reverse it, we get THM{pL4y_w1Th_tH3_URL} which is our final flag!

Ans: THM{pL4y_w1Th_tH3_URL}

Overall a fun room to solve!
